# Checkout rules only. Nothing here rewrites a stored blob.
#
# This file is deliberately narrow. The obvious contents -- a blanket
# `* text=auto eol=lf` -- would renormalise the whole repository, and that
# includes `legacy/`, which is reference material this project promises is
# byte-identical to what it received. `.github/scripts/check_legacy_untouched.sh`
# exists to enforce exactly that, so a .gitattributes that quietly rewrote those
# files would defeat the guard rather than help it.
#
# The rules below mark files as binary or as not-text. That tells Git to write
# the stored bytes to the working tree verbatim, on every platform, and to leave
# the stored bytes alone. Existing blobs are unaffected.

# Reference material: never translated, never renormalised, on any platform.
# Without this a Windows checkout rewrites these to CRLF, every checksum in the
# manifest changes, and the guard fails on a tree nobody has edited -- with the
# only clue being that the single binary file still matches.
legacy/** -text

# The manifest records checksums of the files above, so it has to travel with
# them untranslated or the comparison is against differently-terminated lines.
.github/legacy.sha256 -text

# Shell scripts must reach Linux with LF endings or the kernel reads "\r" as
# part of the interpreter path and the script dies as "bad interpreter". These
# are already stored with LF, so this pins existing behaviour rather than
# changing it, and stops a Windows checkout from committing CRLF back.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary formats, so Git makes no attempt to be clever about them.
*.npz binary
*.zip binary
*.mat binary
*.chk binary
*.fch binary
